# atdmbt

`atdmbt` generates MoonBit types and JSON readers and writers from ATD
files. For `foo.atd`, it creates `foo.mbt` in the current directory; `-o`
chooses another output file, and `-o -` prints the code.

These examples are executed by `moon cram test tests/cram`. With `moonx`,
use `moonx bobzhang/atd/cmd/atdmbt` in place of `atdmbt.exe`.

## Generating code

```mooncram
$ cat > shapes.atd <<'EOF'
> type point = { x : float; ~y <mbt default="1.0"> : float }
> type shape = [ Dot | Circle of (point * float) ] <json repr="object">
> EOF
```

The generated code depends on the runtime package
`bobzhang/atd/runtime`, which the `moon.pkg` of the package containing the
code must import with the alias `atd_runtime`:

```mooncram
$ head -n 16 shapes.mbt
[1]
```

Records become structs with a constructor taking labelled arguments, and
sum types become enums:

```mooncram
$ sed -n '/^pub(all) struct Point/,/^}/p;/^pub fn Point::new/,/^}/p;/^pub(all) enum Shape/,/^}/p' shapes.mbt
[1]
```

Each type has a JSON writer and reader, and convenience functions:

```mooncram
$ grep '^pub fn\|^pub impl' shapes.mbt
[2]
```

The code follows the JSON conventions of ATD: here, the sum type uses
`<json repr="object">`, so `Circle` is written as `{"Circle": ...}`:

```mooncram
$ sed -n '/^pub fn write_shape/,/^}/p' shapes.mbt
[1]
```

## MoonBit-specific annotations

`<mbt ...>` annotations tune the generated code: here, a map, 64-bit
integers, a renamed field and a custom type for a `wrap` construct.

```mooncram
$ cat > options.atd <<'EOF'
> type date = string wrap <mbt t="Date" wrap="Date::parse" unwrap="Date::to_string">
> type account = {
>   id : int <mbt repr="int64">;
>   type_ <mbt name="kind"> : string;
>   created : date;
>   scores : (string * int) list <json repr="object"> <mbt repr="map">;
> }
> EOF
```

## Errors

Unsupported constructs are reported with their location:

```mooncram
$ echo 'type t = t list' > recursive.atd
```
